#!/usr/bin/env python3
# AI-hint: [remote.rdp] RFB bridge + remote-display manager between xrdp's libvnc (RFB 3.3, server-chosen security) and wayvnc/neatvnc (RFB 3.8). Per RDP attach it starts a wayvnc child; mode "virtual" first creates a Hyprland headless output ([remote.rdp].virtual_output, HEADLESS-*) attached below the centre of the primary local output (virtual_attach = "below-center", edge-adjacent in logical px, re-centred on every resize/rotation/scale change; several remote outputs are centred as a group), consumes the client's SetDesktopSize, applies mode + the [desktop.scaling] scale (`mios-gen display-scale --remote`) with hl.monitor to that output only, and answers xrdp itself (wayvnc always runs --disable-resizing: its wlr-output-management path rewrote the physical monitor's rule on Hyprland); a Hyprland config reload (socket2 configreloaded) or another remote output coming/going re-applies that output's mode, scale, position and workspace rule (a reload drops the bridge's runtime rules; no primary local output = placed at 0,0, never at a negative x); the pointer is drawn into the frames (wayvnc --render-cursor, cursor pseudo-encodings dropped from xrdp's SetEncodings) when the output can be scaled ([remote.rdp].render_cursor = "auto"), because wayvnc's cursor capture fails for good on a scaled Hyprland output; mode "mirror" shares a physical output unresized. If wayvnc dies mid-session it is restarted transparently: xrdp's connection is kept, the partial RFB message is completed, the new wayvnc is handshaken by the bridge and asked for a full update. Relays pixels verbatim; frames both RFB directions to inject/rewrite ExtendedDesktopSize replies. Refresh pacing ([remote.rdp].refresh_pacing = "adaptive", Pacer): every client starts at target_hz; when it provably takes fewer than half the frames (e.g. a phone over the RDP link, ~11 frames/s of legacy bitmaps) the virtual output's refresh drops to >= 2x the delivered rate (floor min_hz) so the iGPU stops rendering frames nobody receives, and it returns to target_hz as soon as the client keeps up; delivered rate, refresh and pacing are in stats.json.
# AI-related: /usr/libexec/mios/mios-rdp, /usr/lib/systemd/user/mios-rdp-vnc.service, /usr/share/mios/mios.toml [remote.rdp] [desktop.scaling], /usr/bin/mios-gen (display-scale), /usr/libexec/mios/wayvnc/wayvnc (automation/70-bake-wayvnc-upstream.sh), $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/mios-display-hints.json
# AI-functions: handshake, upstream_handshake, RFBFramingError, ClientStream, ServerStream, eds_update, Hypr, parse_cursorpos, monitor_at, should_restore_local, attach_below, wayvnc_version, scaled_capture_ok, overlay_cursor, policy_scale, write_display_hint, refresh_pacing, Pacer, Bridge, Bridge.reapply, Bridge._set_refresh, Bridge._pace, Bridge._event_action, valid_scale, target_scale, main
"""xrdp (RFB 3.3) <-> wayvnc (RFB 3.8) bridge on UNIX sockets, plus the remote display.

Both sockets live in the operator's 0700 runtime dir; wayvnc's own socket is
never handed to anything but this bridge. Authentication happens in front, in
xrdp-sesman (PAM + group); the RFB hop itself carries security type None.

virtual mode (default): the RDP client gets its own Hyprland output, never the
physical monitor. Lifecycle per attach:
  1. hl.workspace_rule / hl.monitor rules for the output, `hyprctl output create
     headless <name>`, attached below the centre of the primary local output
     (virtual_attach = "below-center"): top edge flush with its bottom edge, so the
     cursor and windows cross the shared edge both ways;
  2. wayvnc -o <name> --disable-resizing: wayvnc's own resize path uses
     wlr-output-management, whose configuration re-lists every head; Hyprland 0.56
     then replaced the PHYSICAL monitor's rule (preferred 60 Hz, auto position);
  3. xrdp's SetDesktopSize (initial size and every DISP resize/rotation) is
     consumed by the bridge, applied with hl.monitor to the virtual output only
     (mode + [desktop.scaling] scale + re-centred position), and answered to xrdp
     (ServerStream);
  3b. wayvnc exiting while xrdp is still connected (e.g. the Fedora-packaged release's fatal cursor-capture
     error on a scaled output) is recovered: the bridge completes the RFB message
     xrdp was receiving, starts a new wayvnc, handshakes it itself, replays xrdp's
     SetPixelFormat/SetEncodings and requests a full update (wayvnc_restarts per minute);
  3c. a Hyprland config reload re-evaluates the projection (catch-all monitor rule
     "preferred, auto, 1") and drops the bridge's runtime hl.monitor/workspace rules: the
     socket2 watcher sees configreloaded and re-applies mode + scale + position (Bridge.reapply,
     debounced and verified); a remote sibling output added/removed re-centres the group;
  4. detach: wayvnc is stopped FIRST (it would otherwise fall back to capturing
     the physical output), then the output is removed after linger_seconds
     unless another attach arrives or keep_virtual_output is set;
  5. refresh pacing (refresh_pacing = "adaptive"): the output starts at target_hz and
     follows the delivered rate per stats window (Pacer) -- a client taking fewer than
     half the frames gets a refresh >= 2x what it takes (floor min_hz), one keeping up
     gets target_hz back at once -- so frames nobody receives are never rendered.

Single seat: remote pointer input moves the one Hyprland cursor onto the remote
display. The local position is saved at attach and handed back once at detach,
only if the remote client sent pointer input and the cursor is still on the
remote display (never per click/hover). hyprctl runs in worker threads only.
"""

